  "articleBody": "Ever wonder how to make sure your kitchen outlets are actually useful for everyone, especially if someone uses a wheelchair? It's not just about meeting code; it's about making daily life easier. A big part of accessible design is ensuring that outlets are reachable from a seated position. This means not placing them too high or too far back on the counter. You've also got to think about the clear floor space in front of the outlet. If a wheelchair user needs to get close, there shouldn't be any obstructions. It's really important to map out each usable counter section and make sure no part is left without proper access. This can get tricky, especially with islands or unique counter shapes, and that's where an expert eye comes in handy. Making sure everything is right before cabinets or backsplashes go in can save a ton of hassle later. If you're planning a kitchen remodel and accessibility is a priority, consider getting professional help to ensure your outlet placement is spot on.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}